Adaptive Traffic Signal System

A Java-based Adaptive Traffic Signal System that dynamically manages traffic signals according to vehicle density on different roads. The system also provides emergency priority and a real-time countdown timer.

Overview

Traditional traffic signal systems generally operate using fixed timings. This project demonstrates a simple adaptive approach where the green signal duration changes according to the number of vehicles waiting on each road.

The system manages four roads:

North

South

East

West

The road with the highest traffic density can be selected for the green signal, while emergency situations can immediately assign priority to a selected road.

Features

Adaptive traffic signal selection

Dynamic green signal timing

Real-time countdown timer

Emergency road priority

Vehicle count management

Input validation

Menu-driven console interface

Multithreading for timer management

How the System Works

The system maintains the number of vehicles on four roads.

When adaptive signal selection is requested:

The system calculates the total number of vehicles.

It compares the vehicle count on each road.

The road with the highest number of vehicles is selected.

Green signal duration is calculated based on traffic density.

Ten vehicles are simulated as passing through the selected road.

The countdown timer starts for the selected green signal.

If an emergency is reported, the selected emergency road immediately receives the green signal.

Green Signal Calculation

The system calculates green signal duration using the following formula:

Green Time = Minimum Time + (Vehicles × Maximum Extra Time / Total Vehicles)


The values used by the program are:

Minimum Time       = 10 seconds
Maximum Extra Time = 30 seconds
Maximum Green Time = 40 seconds


If there are no vehicles on any road, the default green signal duration is 30 seconds.

Emergency Priority

The system allows emergency priority to be assigned to any road.

For example:

Select Emergency Road:

1. North
2. South
3. East
4. West


After selecting a road, the system immediately changes the current green signal to that road.

The program also simulates 10 vehicles passing through the emergency-priority road and resets the signal timer to 30 seconds.

Project Structure
adaptive-traffic-signal/
│
├── Main.java
├── README.md
└── .gitignore

Main Components
Road Class

The Road class represents an individual road.

It stores:

String name;
int vehicles;
boolean emergency;


These properties represent the road name, number of vehicles, and emergency status.

Main Class

The Main class contains the complete traffic signal simulation.

Major methods include:

calculateGreenTime()
getInteger()
updateTraffic()
displayTraffic()
updateTimerOnly()
startTimer()
selectRoad()
emergency()
printMenu()
main()

Menu

The application provides the following options:

1. Update Traffic
2. Display Traffic
3. Select Adaptive Signal
4. Emergency Priority
5. Exit

Update Traffic

Allows the user to enter the number of vehicles on each road.

Example:

Enter North vehicles: 25
Enter South vehicles: 15
Enter East vehicles: 40
Enter West vehicles: 20

Display Traffic

Displays the current traffic information:

North : 25 vehicles
South : 15 vehicles
East  : 40 vehicles
West  : 20 vehicles

Green Signal : East
Time Remaining : 35 seconds

Select Adaptive Signal

The program identifies the road with the highest traffic volume and assigns the green signal to that road.

Emergency Priority

Allows the user to immediately give green-signal priority to a selected road.

Exit

Stops the traffic signal simulation and terminates the program.

